"# Chapter 6 — Hashes and signatures (a dialect-boundary chapter)

When a module system fetches code from elsewhere, it needs to *prove* what that code is. Hashes and signatures are those proofs — a hash is a fact about content, a signature is a proof about an author. This chapter teaches both as real engineering literacy, and marks the truth: **Motoi has no module hashes and no module signatures**, because Motoi fetches no modules. Its standard library ships inside the interpreter, so there is no in-transit code to verify. The verbs below — `module-hash`, `module-signature`, `module-verify`, `trust-add!` — are other dialects' verbs, not Motoi's.

Read this chapter twice, like chapter 5: once for the concepts (they matter wherever code travels), once as a dialect boundary.

## 6.1 The content hash (the concept)

A content hash is a stable, deterministic function of some bytes: `hash(source) → a fixed-length fingerprint`. The same bytes always hash to the same fingerprint; any change — even a comment — produces a different one. That is what makes hashes useful for identity: two things with the same hash are (to overwhelming probability) the same content.

In a module-having Scheme you might call `(module-hash 'manners)` and get 64 hex characters back. **Motoi has no such verb** — `(module-hash 'manners)` yields `error: unbound symbol: module-hash`. There is no module object to hash. What Motoi guarantees instead is simpler: a given interpreter build *is* the library, identically, everywhere it runs. The batteries are the same on every machine because they are the same bytes of interpreter:

```scheme
(list (string-upcase \"same\") (delete-duplicates '(1 1 2)))
;; ⇒ (\"SAME\" (1 2))
```

Those verbs behave identically on any machine running this interpreter build — that is Motoi's reproducibility story, achieved by shipping the library rather than fetching and hashing it.

## 6.2 Why hashes matter

Names can lie and versions get bumped, so in a fetch-based ecosystem only a hash tells you *exactly what code ran*. If a program pins `^1.0.0` and the resolver picks 1.5.3 today and 1.5.4 tomorrow, both meet the pin but they are different code; the hash is the difference.

This whole problem does not arise in Motoi. There is no resolver picking between 1.5.3 and 1.5.4 behind your back, because there is no fetching. The code that ran is the interpreter you launched — full stop.

## 6.3 Hash pins (in the other dialect)

A fetch-based `import` can pin an exact hash — for example `(import manners :hash \"abc123...\")` — demanding that exact content or nothing. Motoi has no `import` and no hash pin. Nothing to pin, nothing to fetch. If you need to record *which interpreter build* your program expects, write that note yourself with Cortex (section 6.10).

## 6.4 The author signature (the concept)

A signature is a cryptographic proof of authorship: an author signs a hash with a private key; anyone with the matching public key can verify it. The hash says *this is the content*; the signature says *this content was blessed by this author*. In the other dialect you might inspect one with `module-signature`.

**Motoi has no `module-signature`** — the standard library has no per-module author to attest, because there are no fetched modules. The idea is worth knowing (signatures are everywhere in software supply chains); it simply has no in-language surface in a batteries-included Scheme.

## 6.5 Why signatures matter

Two reasons, wherever code travels: signatures prevent tampering in transit (verifying the *author's* signature against the *content hash* means no intermediary can substitute code without breaking the signature), and they create accountability (a valid signature is a name you can point to). Signatures create *provenance*, not *trust* — you still decide whom to trust.

None of this machinery lives in Motoi, because Motoi's library does not travel. It is not fetched, not re-signed at hops, not substitutable in transit. The absence of the machinery follows from the absence of the transit.

## 6.6 Verifying a module (in the other dialect)

A fetch-based system verifies signatures on load, with verbs like `module-verify`. Motoi has no `module-verify` (`error: unbound symbol: module-verify`). There is nothing to verify, because nothing arrived from elsewhere. The Motoi analog of \"did I get the code I expected\" is answered by the interpreter version you are running, which you can see and pin at the host level.

## 6.7 The chain: hash, signature, trust

The general model — (1) hash check: does content match the claimed hash? (2) signature check: is the signature valid for that hash under the author's key? (3) trust: do *you* trust this author? — is excellent literacy for any package ecosystem. In Motoi, layers 1 and 2 do not apply (no fetched content, no per-module authors), and layer 3 collapses to a single decision you already made: do you trust this Motoi interpreter build? There is no `trust-add!`/`trust-list` per-author registry, because there is no per-author module supply.

## 6.8 Unsigned modules — not applicable

The other dialect distinguishes signed from unsigned modules and makes you opt in to load an unsigned one (`:trust :unsigned`). Motoi has no signed/unsigned distinction and no `:trust` marker, because it has no modules to sign. Your own code is neither signed nor unsigned in this sense; it is simply the file you run.

## 6.9 Rotating a key — not applicable

Key rotation and revocation matter when authors sign fetched modules. Motoi has no author keys to rotate. This is not a gap; it is a consequence of shipping the library with the interpreter rather than fetching it per author.

## 6.10 What Cortex records

The other dialect logs each verification (`module-verified`) so an auditor can build a bill of materials. Motoi logs no such thing, because it verifies no modules. But Cortex is real, and *your* program can record whatever provenance facts it cares about — for instance, the interpreter version it expects:

```scheme
(cortex/write 'expects-build \"motoi-1.0\")
(cortex/recall 'expects-build)
```

That is the honest Motoi shape of a bill of materials: the facts you decide to write down, recallable later.

## 6.11 Try it

Prove the boundary at a Motoi REPL: type `(module-hash 'x)`, `(module-signature 'x)`, and `(module-verify 'x)` and read three `unbound symbol` errors. Then run a string verb and a list verb (section 6.1) and note they behave identically to how they behave on any other machine running this build — Motoi's reproducibility comes from *shipping* the library, not from *hashing a fetched* one.

## 6.12 The point

Hashes and signatures are the tools a *fetch-based* ecosystem uses to answer \"what code ran, who wrote it, can I prove it wasn't tampered with.\" Motoi answers those questions a different way: it does not fetch. The library is the interpreter; the interpreter is a known build; the build is the same everywhere. Learn the hash/signature model — you will need it in the wider world — and understand that Motoi's simplicity is *why* it needs none of it here.

Turn the page. Chapter 7 covers composing small pieces into one working program — a skill that is entirely real in Motoi, using plain `define`.")
